+## How the outer loop is actually solved
+
+That loop is Richardson iteration.  The sweep never reads the incoming
+distribution -- it is an exact per-direction solve, visited upwind-first --
+so everything it needs from the previous iterate is the scattering source:
+`(T_s, qx_s, qy_s)` per element DOF, plus the emission of any diffusely
+reflecting wall.  Call that vector `x`; one iteration is the affine map
+
+    x <- K x + c
+
+and the converged state is the solution of the linear system `(I - K) x = c`.
+The rate of the plain loop is the spectral radius of `K`, which goes to 1 in
+both stiff limits -- optically thick (`tau_R -> 0`) and hydrodynamic
+(`tau_N << tau_R`) -- which is why the shipped loop needs 17 000 sweeps at
+`tau_R = 1e-2` and never arrives at `1e-3`.
+
+`pybte.accel` solves the same system with GMRES instead, preconditioned by a
+*low-order* copy of the discrete problem: same mesh, same DG space, same
+collision operator, same boundary treatment, but three to five discrete
+ordinates instead of two hundred.  The coarse ordinate set is built to
+reproduce `INT dOmega`, `INT c dOmega` and `INT c c dOmega` exactly, so the
+low-order model has the same diffusion limit and the same momentum
+relaxation rate as the problem it accelerates; it is small enough and sparse
+enough to LU-factorise once at setup and apply directly.  That is classical
+synthetic acceleration (DSA), with the low-order model built by angular
+coarsening rather than by hand.
+
+Nothing in it touches the operator or the right-hand side, so the answer is
+unchanged -- what changes is that every case now converges in 13 to 25
+sweeps, and that "converged" means the residual of `(I - K) x - c` is at
+round-off rather than that successive iterates stopped moving.  Those are
+different statements: a step of `1e-8` at `rho = 1 - 1e-3` leaves an error of
+`1e-5`.
+
+`scheme.accelerate: false` restores the Fortran's loop verbatim.
+
